WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.780
On the north-east coast of Germany,
looking out on the Baltic Sea,

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.669
there’s a tourist attraction
that I think is really strange.

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:09.130
Four of them, actually, all identical
at different seaside resorts.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:12.210
And I think they’re strange
because they’re modern.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.330
The Tauchgondel, the diving gondola,
feels like it should be

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.800
some 19th-century
Industrial Revolution invention.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.980
It’s a diving bell: a chamber that
sinks under the water

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.039
and lets you look out
on the ocean floor.

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:25.189
Which is only a couple of metres
down at this point, sure,

00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:28.610
but this isn’t like the Disneyland
fake-submarines that pretend to dive

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:30.570
using some bubbles and lighting tricks:

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.870
this really does go underwater.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:33.920
To me, this feels like it should be

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.610
some sort of overcomplicated,
steam-powered thing

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.660
that’d entertain rich Bismarck-era
royalty for a few years,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.460
and then fall into disrepair
and turn up in a few old photos

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.850
of odd bizarre seaside attractions
from the past.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:48.160
But this?
It opened in 2009.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.160
(IN GERMAN):
 
 
 
 

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.300
The technology here is modern.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.930
And with modern technology comes
modern safety standards.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.720
This gondola has the opposite problem
to most elevators:

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.730
the average tourist isn’t going to be
worried that it might fall,

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.200
but they are going to ask if it
might get stuck down there.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.275
Or worse, that it might, uh...

00:01:21.275 --> 00:01:23.183
...spring a leak.

00:01:23.836 --> 00:01:25.969
[horn sounds]

00:01:26.465 --> 00:01:29.646
[mechanical noises]

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:51.990
Ha! Okay.
I did not expect that.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:58.360
Just... having a wave right
in your face is weird.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.468
I didn't expect the sound.

00:02:13.468 --> 00:02:16.842
I don't know why. But I
didn't expect the sound.

00:02:16.842 --> 00:02:19.280
[deep, rumbling waves]

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.710
There is one slight catch, of course:

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.860
there’s not too much usually
visible in the Baltic Sea.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.560
It’s not crystal-clear Caribbean water
on the best of days,

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.270
and today all those waves are
churning up the ocean bed as well.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.820
So if you were hoping that sharks

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.110
or huge schools of tropical fish
would swim by,

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:38.640
you’re going to be disappointed.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.530
You might get a couple
of curious flounders,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.440
or a few jellyfish passing by.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:43.440
The novelty is being on

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.720
what is, to be fair, an actual
short diving expedition

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.220
without needing scuba equipment
or a wetsuit.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.620
But the folks here were worried that
people might be disappointed if

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.080
they didn’t see anything...

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.697
so they actually close the
shutters and show a 3D film.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.780
If the goal was “see the ocean floor”,

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:18.030
well, you could do that without the chamber
and all the fancy mechanisms:

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.230
you could set down a few big walls
with thick windows in them,

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.370
pump the water out, and just have
a regular viewing gallery

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.430
that you could stand in.

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:26.600
But that’s not as cool.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.260
It is a little unnerving seeing
the ocean rise around you,

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:31.500
out of your control,

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.910
and it’s also a bit reassuring
to surface again into the sunshine.

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:37.540
Because in the end,
it’s not about the fish.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:38.720
It’s about the experience.

